[38;5;231m⏺[39m Approach A, unit tests only. Here's the design — first half. Flag anything that looks wrong before I continue.

  [1mSection 1: Architecture and components[0m

  Five small files under [38;5;153mlogging/[39m, split so that everything security-relevant or logic-bearing is pure and testable
  without a DOM, a network, or a process:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogging/core.js[0m — [38;5;153mcreateLogger({ level, transport, context })[39m returns [38;5;153m{ debug, info, warn, error, child }[39m. Pure: it
    builds an event, applies the level filter, runs redaction, hands the result to a transport. No globals, no I/O, no
    knowledge of which runtime it's in.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogging/redact.js[0m — the allowlist. Isolated in its own file because it's the piece that keeps a password out of a
    payload, and it should be readable and tested on its own terms.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogging/transport.js[0m — the seam. A transport is just [38;5;153m{ send(event) }[39m. Ships [38;5;153mcreateConsoleTransport()[39m and
    [38;5;153mmultiTransport([...])[39m for fan-out. This is the file the vendor never gets to cross.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogging/browser.js[0m — [38;5;153minitBrowserLogging(config)[39m: installs [38;5;153mwindow.addEventListener('error')[39m and
    [38;5;153m('unhandledrejection')[39m, wraps the vendor's CDN global in a transport, returns a logger.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogging/node.js[0m — [38;5;153minitNodeLogging(config)[39m: installs [38;5;153mprocess.on('uncaughtException')[39m and [38;5;153m('unhandledRejection')[39m,
    wraps the npm SDK in a transport, returns a logger.


